{"probe_id": "e2-5401-0", "arm": "A", "run": 1, "model": "gpt-oss-120b|e2v2-armA", "answer": {"value": "In virtually every modern automotive application a three\u2011wire Hall\u2011effect crank\u2011 or cam\u2011shaft position sensor is powered from a regulated 5\u202fV rail that the ECU supplies on the sensor\u2019s VCC pin. The sensor\u2019s signal line then swings between ground and that 5\u202fV reference, so the ECU\u2019s reference voltage for the sensor is essentially the same 5\u202fV supply (typically held within \u00b15\u202f% \u2013 roughly 4.75\u202fV to 5.25\u202fV). \n\nA minority of older or specialty designs use the vehicle\u2019s 12\u202fV battery voltage (or a 12\u202fV\u2011derived rail) as the sensor supply, but the overwhelming majority of current\u2011generation Hall\u2011effect position sensors are 5\u202fV\u2011powered. In those 12\u202fV\u2011based designs the ECU still provides a ground reference and the sensor\u2019s output toggles between ground and the 12\u202fV rail.\n\nSo, the typical reference voltage(s) the ECU supplies are:\n\u2022\u202f5\u202fV (standard for most modern Hall\u2011effect engine\u2011position sensors, regulated to ~4.75\u20115.25\u202fV)\n\u2022\u202f12\u202fV (used only on a few legacy or high\u2011voltage\u2011tolerant sensors)\n\nThe ECU also provides the common ground reference for the sensor\u2019s signal line.", "must_retrieve": false}, "expected_value": "5 or 12", "unit": "volts", "kind": "recall", "tolerance_pct": 1.0, "retrieved_doc_ids": [], "latency_s": 107.89, "completion_tokens": 2387, "prompt_tokens": 147, "finish_reason": "stop", "n_expected": 69, "guard_active": false, "retrieval_mode": null, "retrieval_mode_used": "none", "top_k": 0, "index_mtime": null, "index_stale": null, "dense_fallback": null, "missing_rowids": null, "scorer_version": "2.0", "class": "exact"}
